00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
this morning john berko the former house  of commons speaker has  defected to the labour party a  conservative since his youth he served  as a conservative mp from 1997  until 2009 before occupying the  speaker's chair for a decade  government sources have told sky news  they're not surprised by the defection  after bitter rouse over brexit  made him many enemies in his former  party  well for his first tv interview about  the decision i'm joined here in studio  by john burko trevor good morning to you  good morning um 25 years  a long time why leave now  after all that time well i was a member  of the conservative party for 29 years  but robert buckland spoke truer than he  knew  when he said that i left the  conservative party a long time ago  let's be crystal clear explicit  and accurate trevor i became speaker on  the 22nd of june 2009  and that evening in conformity with  convention i wrote to the then chairman  of the conservative party eric pickles  to tender my resignation as a member of  the conservative party  why because the speaker does not belong  to is  independent of separate from unconnected  with it  no support for operations that's a  different decision any political party  it's a different decision you could have  rejoined when you stepped down  as speaker 18 months ago why didn't i  could have rejoined the conservative  party i didn't have the slightest desire  to do so and i  want to be fundamentally  clear and for there to be no dubai about  this  as i said yesterday to an observer  journalist  my values are left of center i do not  identify with the conservative party  and i think that in british politics  you've just heard from ed davey  most people still see it as principally  a choice between the conservative party  and the labour party  and the labour party is the alternative  government  to the conservative party if you believe  as i do  that this government is bad news unfit  to govern and that the prime minister  though a successful campaigner is a  lousy governor  you have to ask yourself do you want rid  of it and if you want rid of it  you have then to pose to yourself the  question and to answer it  how is that to be achieved and the  reality of the matter is in  the united kingdom the only credible  vehicle for the  removal of this government  is the election of a labor government  committed equality  social justice internationalism and a  fairer  and more inclusive society that's the  labor brand i know you're a person who  chooses your words very carefully john  you you've used as expression this  governments  uh i think three or four times this  morning at what point did you decide  you couldn't be a conservative was it  when the conservative party  was taken over by boris johnson or was  it something that changed in you  no it was something that changed in me  and i think oh so it's not boring i owe  it to myself  and to you and to everybody else to be  on the record about it and for there to  be no  uncertainty so if you're saying to me is  it principally about boris johnson the  honest answer that is  no and in fact there has been i think  already some confusion and  misrepresentation of that point  it's not personal against boris johnson  i do think that  he is someone who has only a nodding  acquaintance with the truth in elite  year  and i think that the utter contempt with  which he has treated parliament is  lamentable  and i think it has exacerbated the very  strong feelings of  resentment towards him because i think a  lot of people feel that's not the way to  behave  telling the truth in and to parliament  matters circumventing parliament  is wrong treating parliament with  disdain is objectionable  but no i have over a long period evolved  my political thinking  i wasn't a member of a party throughout  my tenure tenure  as speaker because it would have been  quite wrong to be  i didn't take a view i sought to  facilitate the house of commons to  express  its view and all individual members to  put their points across because that's  the responsibility  of the speaker now i'm a private citizen  as robert buckland says  i'm entitled to take a political view  and my view is a left-of-center view  i identify with labor values labour  principles labour policies i'm going to  come to labor in a second but  um you've used the words xenophobic  nationalistic you have not just said  some  let's say less than fond things about  boris johnson it's the party you don't  like isn't it  and you don't like conservatives anymore  well i don't dislike  individual conservatives i have still a  number of conservative friends and i can  admire  and respect very much trevor people  across the political spectrum with a  vast miscellany of different views  on the understanding that they're honest  and principled and standing up  for the principles in which they believe  so it's not a question of personalities  it's a question of the issues but if you  say do i like the conservative party  the answer is no and i'm absolutely  clear  that i am not these days a conservative  so it's not  about boris johnson as tony benn used to  say it's not about personalities  it's about policies all right um you  know what they're saying aren't they  boris johnson didn't give you a period  and kiersten will  now give that up well i can answer that  very simply i've had absolutely no  discussion whatsoever either with kia  stammer  or any other member of the labour  leadership about that matter there has  been no barter no trade  no deal whatsoever and if i may very  politely say so  and i do the people who make what they  think  is that potent and coruscating criticism  of me  are operating according to their own  rather low standards let's deal with the  issue rather than impugn people's  motives it's got  nothing to do with that whatsoever  before before we leave that  leader of the opposition conceivable you  might be watching this program  it might be occurring to him right now i  must bring up john and offer him a  period if he were to do that  what would you say well i think there's  a lot to be said trevor  for the old willy white law  adage personally i think it is best to  cross bridges  only when one comes to them it isn't in  my mind it's not part of the game plan i  haven't discussed it i'm not waiting for  it  what i'm motivated by is a commitment to  equality  social justice and internationalism i  believe  absolutely passionately and these are  the issues  that on leveling up the government  mouths the mantra  but it doesn't know the meaning of the  mantra if you  look at the recent record the  government's own education czar said 15  000 million pounds is needed to level up  and the government has offered less than  one-tenth of that  let's focus on the issues slashing the  aid budget  it's wrong it's objectionable utterly  shameful  we talk about these things a lot on this  program but um  you know we have the excitement of  having you here this morning so let's  talk about  you um it could be said  that this is a typical berko grand  gesture  to avoid speculation  it's part of your legacy uh of the  criticism  of you uh as presiding over a culture of  bullying in the house  houses of parliament how do you respond  to that  that this is not really what you're  about what you're really trying to do  is to bury all of that i'm not trying to  bury anything  i think the suggestion that i presided  over that culture  is quite wrong i'm not going to talk  about individual instances  because there is a confidentiality about  that process which shouldn't be  compromised  or disrespected in any way i can just  say however there was  one individual who brought a complaint  of bullying against me  it was investigated and it was rejected  but i put to you again trevor and i  appreciate you've got your journalistic  responsibility  let's focus on the issues as far as i'm  concerned  issues that's what caused me to make  photos  um speak honestly to me  i always do do you can you say that you  never did anything that you felt now  you regret or you thought might have  been objectionable  or that might have been something that  when you look back now you think  i wish i hadn't done that oh in relation  to this issue  of bullying and so on gosh i would have  to cast my mind back we all make  mistakes  have i been perfect in every instance  can i say  that with the benefit of high insight  i'd never have done something  differently of course i couldn't but i  come back to the central proposition  one has as a private citizen the right  to make a judgment about which political  parties are support  i believe in terms of values the labour  party is preferable to the conservative  party and as far as kierstama is  concerned i don't know  whether he's going to make it  politically or not but i think he's an  honest decent intelligent person who has  come into politics  as the continuation of public service  and the real issue in my mind is who  has the vision of a more equitable  society who  thirsts to deliver social mobility who  wants to better the lot of people  less fortunate than a political leader  on on  that calculus is vastly preferable  tomorrow you've talked about labour's  values and policies  i have to confess i've been doing this  program for some weeks now  i've yet to drag anything that looked  like a policy out of a labour  spokesperson  what is the policy or value that you  think labor has that really offers this  i'll give you two examples one domestic  and one international  a domestic commitment to leveling up on  the proper funding  of education so that those who have been  dramatically damaged as a consequence of  the pandemic have the chance to recover  that's the first  example and the second example which  speaks to the dna of a progressive party  is that the labour party thinks it is  utterly wrong  and immoral to slash the aid budget  cutting provision for the poorest and  most destitute people on the face of the  planet who don't live  on a dollar a day they exist on a dollar  a day  okay that is wrong okay period i'm  afraid i have to ask pretty much  everybody  who um has experienced government and  been  seen in conservative party this today um  matt hancock  completely useless or what well i think  it would be fair for me to say  that i would buy matt at  my valuation and sell him at his  and realize a healthy profit in the  process  i think i've worked out exactly what  that means  well we'll see how hancock's fair thank  you very much  uh sean berker trevor thank you very  much indeed good morning to you

